[5,4,3,2,3,1,2],[5,4,3,2,3,2,1],[5,4,3,3,1,2,2],[5,4,3,3,2,1,2],[5,4,3,3,2,2,1]]}],"solution":{"explanation":"<p>The template: choose, recurse, un-choose. Duplicates are prevented at generation time by a canonical-order rule: among equal values, they must be placed left to right, so skip nums[i] when nums[i \u2212 1] is equal and unused. Generating everything and deduplicating with a set is correct but wastes up to n! work.</p>","python":"def permute_unique(nums):\n    nums = sorted(nums); out = []; used = [False] * len(nums); cur = []\n    def bt():\n        if len(cur) == len(nums): out.append(cur[:]); return\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\n            if used[i] or (i and nums[i] == nums[i - 1] and not used[i - 1]): continue\n            used[i] = True; cur.append(nums[i]); bt(); cur.pop(); used[i] = False\n    bt(); return out\n","cpp":"vector<vector<int>> out_; vector<int> cur_; vector<bool> used_;\nvoid bt_(vector<int>& a) {\n    if (cur_.size() == a.size()) { out_.push_back(cur_); return; }\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); i++) { if (used_[i] || (i && a[i] == a[i-1] && !used_[i-1])) continue; used_[i] = true; cur_.push_back(a[i]); bt_(a); cur_.pop_back(); used_[i] = false; }\n}\nvector<vector<int>> permuteUnique(vector<int>& nums) { sort(nums.begin(), nums.end()); out_.clear(); cur_.clear(); used_.assign(nums.size(), false); bt_(nums); return out_; }\n"}}